---

## Identification

The group is the **finite Heisenberg group over $\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}$**, denoted $H(\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z})$ — equivalently, the **Weyl–Heisenberg group** (or **generalized Pauli group**) of order $N^3$. For $N=2$ it is the dihedral group $D_4$ of order $8$, which is an extraspecial $2$-group of $+$ type and hence a "sibling" of the quaternion group $Q_8$ (the $-$ type).

---

## Proof

### 1. Setup and the commutation relation

Let $\omega = e^{2\pi i/N}$. The intended diagonal matrix (the only choice consistent with the stated relation $AB=\omega\,BA$) is the **clock/phase operator**

$$A = \operatorname{diag}(1,\,\omega,\,\omega^2,\,\dots,\,\omega^{N-1}),$$

and $B$ is the **cyclic shift** $(Be_j = e_{j+1 \bmod N})$. (The notation "$\operatorname{diag}(A)=(\omega,\omega,\omega,\dots)$" in the problem is shorthand for the powers of $\omega$; a literal scalar matrix $\omega I$ would commute with $B$ and could not satisfy $AB=\omega BA$ for $N>1$.)

**Verification of $AB=\omega BA$.** For each basis vector $e_j$:

$$AB\,e_j = A\,e_{j+1} = \omega^{j+1}\,e_{j+1}, \qquad BA\,e_j = B(\omega^j e_j) = \omega^j\,e_{j+1}.$$

Hence $AB\,e_j = \omega\cdot BA\,e_j$ for all $j$, i.e. $AB = \omega\,BA$. $\checkmark$

Both generators have order $N$: $A^N = I$ (each diagonal entry is an $N$th root of unity) and $B^N = I$ (an $N$-cycle applied $N$ times).

### 2. The commutator is a central scalar

From $AB = \omega\,BA$ we get $BA = \omega^{-1}AB$, so

$$ABA^{-1}B^{-1} = \omega\, I.$$

The commutator $[A,B] = \omega I$ is a **scalar matrix**, hence central in $\mathrm{GL}_N(\mathbb{C})$ and in particular in $G=\langle A,B\rangle$. It generates a central subgroup $Z = \langle \omega I\rangle \cong \mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}$ (since $\omega$ is a primitive $N$th root).

### 3. Normal form: every element is $\omega^m A^a B^b$

The relation $BA = \omega^{-1}AB$ lets us push every $B$ past every $A$ at the cost of a power of $\omega$. By induction,

$$B^b A^a = \omega^{-ab}\, A^a B^b \qquad (a,b\in\mathbb{Z}).$$

Therefore any word in $A, B, A^{-1}, B^{-1}$ can be rewritten (collecting all $\omega$-factors to the front, all $A$-powers next, all $B$-powers last) as

$$\omega^m\, A^a\, B^b, \qquad m,a,b \in \mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}.$$

So $G = \{\,\omega^m A^a B^b : m,a,b \in \mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}\,\}$, giving $|G| \le N^3$.

### 4. Uniqueness of the normal form $\Rightarrow$ $|G| = N^3$

The matrix $\omega^m A^a B^b$ is a **monomial matrix**: $B^b$ applies the cyclic permutation $j\mapsto j+b$, and $A^a$ scales row $i$ by $\omega^{ai}$. Concretely,

$$(\omega^m A^a B^b)_{ij} = \begin{cases} \omega^{m+ai} & \text{if } i \equiv j+b \pmod N,\\ 0 & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}$$

Suppose $\omega^m A^a B^b = \omega^{m'} A^{a'} B^{b'}$.

- **Same permutation** $\Rightarrow$ $b \equiv b' \pmod N$.
- **Row $i=0$ entry**: $\omega^m = \omega^{m'}$ $\Rightarrow$ $m\equiv m'$.
- **Row $i=1$ entry**: $\omega^{m+a} = \omega^{m'+a'}$ $\Rightarrow$ $a\equiv a'$.

Hence $(m,a,b) = (m',a',b')$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z})^3$, the normal form is unique, and

$$\boxed{|G| = N^3.}$$

This confirms the stated lower bound "order at least $N^3$" — in fact the order is **exactly** $N^3$.

### 5. Multiplication law and isomorphism with the Heisenberg group

Using $B^b A^{a'} = \omega^{-a'b}A^{a'}B^b$, the product of two normal forms is

$$(\omega^m A^a B^b)(\omega^{m'} A^{a'} B^{b'}) = \omega^{\,m+m'-a'b}\, A^{a+a'}\, B^{b+b'}.$$

The **finite Heisenberg group** is

$$H(\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}) = \left\{\begin{pmatrix} 1 & a & c \\ 0 & 1 & b \\ 0 & 0 & 1\end{pmatrix} : a,b,c \in \mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}\right\},$$

with multiplication $(c,a,b)\cdot(c',a',b') = (c+c'+ab',\; a+a',\; b+b')$.

**The map**

$$\Phi:\; \omega^m A^a B^b \;\longmapsto\; \begin{pmatrix} 1 & a & m+ab \\ 0 & 1 & b \\ 0 & 0 & 1\end{pmatrix}$$

is a group isomorphism. Indeed, it sends the generators correctly:

$$A \mapsto \begin{pmatrix}1&1&0\\0&1&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix} =: x, \quad B \mapsto \begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\0&1&1\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix} =: y, \quad \omega I \mapsto \begin{pmatrix}1&0&1\\0&1&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix} =: z,$$

and in $H(\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z})$ one checks $xy = z\cdot yx$ (the upper-right entry of $xy$ is $1\cdot 1 = 1$ more than that of $yx$), matching $AB = \omega I \cdot BA$. A direct computation confirms $\Phi$ respects the full multiplication law:

$$\Phi(\text{product}) = \begin{pmatrix}1 & a+a' & (m+m'-a'b)+(a+a')(b+b')\\0&1&b+b'\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}1 & a+a' & m+m'+ab+ab'+a'b'\\0&1&b+b'\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix},$$

$$\Phi(\omega^m A^a B^b)\cdot\Phi(\omega^{m'} A^{a'} B^{b'}) = \begin{pmatrix}1&a&m+ab\\0&1&b\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1&a'&m'+a'b'\\0&1&b'\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}1&a+a'&m+m'+ab+a'b'+ab'\\0&1&b+b'\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}.$$

These are equal (the $-a'b$ and $+a'b$ terms cancel). Since $\Phi$ is a bijective homomorphism between groups of order $N^3$,

$$G \;\cong\; H(\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}). \qquad\blacksquare$$

### 6. Structure summary

| Property | Value |
|---|---|
| Order | $N^3$ |
| Center $Z(G)$ | $\langle \omega I\rangle \cong \mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}$, order $N$ |
| Commutator subgroup $[G,G]$ | $= Z(G) = \langle \omega I\rangle$ |
| Abelianization $G/G'$ | $\cong (\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z})^2$, generated by images of $A, B$ |
| Commutator pairing | $[A^a, B^b] = \omega^{ab}I$ (standard symplectic form on $(\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z})^2$) |
| Exponent | $N$ when $N$ is odd; $2N$-divisible elements appear when $N$ is even |

For **prime** $N=p$, this is the (unique) **extraspecial $p$-group** of order $p^3$ and exponent $p$ (for odd $p$); for $p=2$ it is the extraspecial $2$-group of **$+$ type**.

### 7. The case $N=2$

Here $\omega = -1$, $A = \operatorname{diag}(1,-1) = \sigma_z$, $B = \begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix} = \sigma_x$, and $AB = -BA$. The eight elements are

$$\{\, \pm I,\; \pm\sigma_x,\; \pm\sigma_z,\; \pm(-i\sigma_y)\,\}$$

(since $\sigma_x\sigma_z = -i\sigma_y$). Their orders:

| element | $I$ | $\sigma_x$ | $\sigma_z$ | $-i\sigma_y$ | $-I$ | $-\sigma_x$ | $-\sigma_z$ | $i\sigma_y$ |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| order | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ |

Five elements of order $2$ and two of order $4$ — this is the **dihedral group $D_4$** (symmetries of the square), which is $H(\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z})$.

**Relation to the quaternion group.** $D_4$ and $Q_8$ are the two nonabelian groups of order $8$, and they are precisely the two **extraspecial $2$-groups** of order $8$:

- $D_4$: $+$ type (our group, $H(\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z})$),
- $Q_8$: $-$ type.

Both share the key structural features: center $=$ commutator subgroup $=$ Frattini subgroup $\cong \mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$, and abelianization $\cong (\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z})^2$. They differ only in how many elements of order $4$ they contain ($D_4$ has $2$; $Q_8$ has $6$). This is the precise sense in which the problem's "$N=2$ group is similar to the quaternion group": **they are the two extraspecial groups of order $8$**, arising as the $N=2$ instances of the two families of extraspecial $p$-groups, both governed by the same Heisenberg-type commutator relation $AB = \omega\,BA$.

---

**Conclusion.** The group generated by the clock operator $A=\operatorname{diag}(1,\omega,\dots,\omega^{N-1})$ and the shift operator $B$ with $AB=\omega\,BA$ ($\omega=e^{2\pi i/N}$) is the **finite Heisenberg group $H(\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z})$** of order $N^3$, isomorphic to the group of upper-unitriangular $3\times3$ matrices over $\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}$. For $N=2$ it is the extraspecial $2$-group $D_4$ of $+$ type, the sibling of $Q_8$ (the $-$ type) among the extraspecial groups of order $8$.

